<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Israel, Yaʿaqōv</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob, later given the name Israel, is the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> patriarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> as a son of Isaac and Rebecca and the twin brother of Esau. According to Genesis he bought Esau's birthright and deceived his aging father into blessing him instead of Esau, then went to his uncle Laban in Haran, seeing on the way a vision of a ladder to heaven at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a>. He married Laban's daughters Leah and Rachel, and his sons were each the forefather of one of the Twelve Tribes of Israel. Genesis has him die in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> at the age of 147; mainstream archaeologists agree that no evidence establishes him as a historical figure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacob.jpg" alt="Jacob" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Victors · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Victors_-_Jacob_Seeking_the_Forgiveness_of_Esau_-_79.330_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
